{"authored" [{"question" "q-9457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/state.json" "file_sha256" "7becc90cb0fd23a99b2458b71a48b4031cc64b5450cfe699b8717248b42ac003" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0bf8dc005ff807fc386e7a92f1d75a0fa0012b80de133fc69e1f49058d9876b" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-9457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/state.json" "file_sha256" "7becc90cb0fd23a99b2458b71a48b4031cc64b5450cfe699b8717248b42ac003" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-16647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2}=72$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/state.json" "file_sha256" "7becc90cb0fd23a99b2458b71a48b4031cc64b5450cfe699b8717248b42ac003" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-16647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm \\sqrt{18} \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(3-3\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(3+3\\sqrt{2},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/state.json" "file_sha256" "7becc90cb0fd23a99b2458b71a48b4031cc64b5450cfe699b8717248b42ac003" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-16647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0bf8dc005ff807fc386e7a92f1d75a0fa0012b80de133fc69e1f49058d9876b" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verific