ategory" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/state.json" "file_sha256" "7becc90cb0fd23a99b2458b71a48b4031cc64b5450cfe699b8717248b42ac003" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(0)-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/0a1303644a54b07f874df4f6694d84384e634403bc4efe8ee5086d922bc6f25e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/state.json" "file_sha256" "7becc90cb0fd23a99b2458b71a48b4031cc64b5450cfe699b8717248b42ac003" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b0bf8dc005ff807fc386e7a92f1d75a0fa0012b80de133fc69e1f49058d9876b" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327146" "field" "field-