ategory" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/example-5214.json" "file_sha256" "60313886cdad0855f560f74c2b2754c810ce404fbc071035fbedad195b39a16f" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/example-5220.json" "file_sha256" "fc5bc4e2959d0ede1de83f1b691b44938bd3149fb90a82da94d393c89cae28ed" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $5x^2 + 2y^2 - 20x - 8y + 13 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/example-5221.json" "file_sha256" "ea2beb740b333485cbbf7b694c8527eb888ccaeb8413726ea7a581366018fefd" "basis" 635 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-16647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2}=72$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm \\sqrt{18} \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(3-3\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(3+3\\sqrt{2},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-16647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)"] ["math" "(3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)"] ["math" "(\\pm 3,0)"] ["math" "(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)"] ["text" "There is no $x$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-7643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-7643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{3}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+12 & =16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-7643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-7643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-7643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\pm 2,0)"] ["math" "(\\pm 3,0)"] ["text" "$(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-7643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0)$ and $(4,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9457" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-9457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$"] ["text" "$(-4,0)$ and $(-2,0)$"] ["text" "$(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0)$"] ["text" "$(2,0)$ and $(4,0)$"] ["text" "$(3,0)$ and $(5,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9457" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-16642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)"] ["math" "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0)"] ["math" "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)"] ["math" "(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)"] ["text" "There is no $x$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points if any, where the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2}+20=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(0-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(16) & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+32 & =-20 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{52}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{52}{5}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-16646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\pm 1,0)"] ["math" "(\\pm 4,0)"] ["text" "$(-1,0)$ and $(4,0)$"] ["text" "$(1,0)$ and $(4,0)$"] ["text" "There is no $x$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is no $x$-intercept"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **left to right**, the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+50x-4y=50$ intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+50x-4y & =50 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+(0)^{2}+50x-4(0) & =50 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+50x & =50 \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x & =10 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+10x+25)-25 & =10 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25 & =10 \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =35 \\\\ x+5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{35} \\\\ x & =-5\\pm \\sqrt{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(-5-\\sqrt{35},0)\\,\\text{and}\\,B(-5+\\sqrt{35},0).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5-\\sqrt{35}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5+\\sqrt{35}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-274480" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **left to right**, the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x+4y=28$ intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)\\,$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x+4y & =28 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(0)^{2}+12x+4(0) & =28 \\\\ {x}^{2}+12x & =28 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+12x+36)-36 & =28 \\\\ (x+6)^{2}-36 & =28 \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =64 \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm 8 \\\\ x & =-6\\pm 8 \\\\ x & =-14,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(-14,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,B(2,0).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sh