ion-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328497" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328497" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-72914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-72914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-4x-2(0)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{3},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-72914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-72914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-72914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)"] ["math" "(-3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)"] ["math" "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)"] ["math" "(5,0)"] ["text" "There is no $x$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-72914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+6x+6y=3$  intercepts the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+6x+6y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}+6x+6\\cdot 0 & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-9 & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =3+9 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(-3-2\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(-3+2\\sqrt{3},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-9461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3},0)"] ["math" "(1\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)"] ["math" "(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)"] ["text" "$(-3,0)$ and $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$(-5,0)$ and $(2,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3},0)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-326042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+12x+24y+9=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-326042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+12x+24y+9 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6\\cdot {0}^{2}+12x+24\\cdot 0+9 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+12x+9 & =0 \\\\ (2x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(-\\frac{3}{2},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-326042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-326042" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-326042" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-326042" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-326042" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\frac{2}{5})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ 256+25(y+2)^{2} & =400 \\\\ 25(y+2)^{2} & =144 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{144}{25} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{12}{5} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{12}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{22}{5},\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\frac{2}{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,-\\frac{22}{5}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328527" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{22}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+2)}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{(0+2)}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 12+{(y-3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-3)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-3)^{2}=-3.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-9467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,-1)"] ["math" "(0,1)"] ["text" "$(0,-2)$ and $(0,3)$"] ["text" "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,2)$"] ["text" "There is no $y$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-9467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There is no $y$-intercept"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 1+\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{36} & =0 \\\\ y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-6).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/assets/d3780834bd9f303ee9d7a6fff0c1fcf28ccfff4cbe4d69c9ec38691a33aca7e1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-16684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,-6)"] ["math" "(0,3)"] ["text" "$(0,-3)$ and $(0,6)$"] ["text" "$(0,-6)$ and $(0,3)$"] ["text" "There is no $y$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,-6)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{(y-7)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 81+16(y-7)^{2} & =9\\cdot 16 \\\\ 81+16(y-7)^{2} & =144 \\\\ 16(y-7)^{2} & =63 \\\\ (y-7)^{2} & =\\frac{63}{16} \\\\ y-7 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{16}} \\\\ y-7 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4} \\\\ y & =7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,7-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4})$ and $(0,7+\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-16683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,-7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})"] ["math" "(0,7)"] ["math" "(0,7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4})"] ["math" "(0,\\pm 7)"] ["text" "There is no $y$-intercept"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-16683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,7\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{4})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,-\\frac{1}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9\\,(\\frac{3}{4})+(y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{27}{4}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{36}{4} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,\\,-\\frac{7}{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a00