multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-8y+11=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)\\,$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ [{y}^{2}-8y]+11 & =0 \\\\ [(y-4)^{2}-16]+11 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2}-5 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA\\,(0,4+\\sqrt{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B\\,(0,4-\\sqrt{5}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8b6e6702474aa5857a83fcd72d8cc30f47f7f1e29cb93ff75397ea8c9ed6925c"} {"question" "q-328517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ff6f25355f5ba1b63a008467791aa46d3322718ec7dc41d87d5b384ba372a02f"} {"question" "q-328517" "field" "fi